  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nGusty westerly winds behind a departing cold front will subside\nshortly after sunset with the loss of daytime heating. Surface \nhigh pressure building across the northern Great Lakes will bring \ndry and tranquil weather for tonight and Wednesday. Radiational \ncooling beneath clear to partly cloudy skies and light winds will \npromote the formation of patchy ground fog late tonight into early\nWednesday morning, particularly across far northern Wisconsin. \nHigh temperatures Wednesday will be seasonable, reaching the upper\n60s to low 70s. \n\nAs the frontal zone slowly returns north Wednesday night, \nelevated warm advection will bring a 30 to 50 percent chance of \nlight showers to the region. However, moisture availability is \nmodest and elevated instability reaches only 100 to 200 J/kg, \nkeeping the probability of rainfall exceeding 0.50 inches under 10\nto 20 percent, with no heavy rain impacts anticipated.\n\nA more dynamic weather system is forecast to impact north-central\nand northeast Wisconsin late Friday into Saturday as a shortwave\nejects from the western United States trough. Supported by a\n40 to 50 knot low-level jet and precipitable water values rising\nto around 1.70 inches (roughly 95 percent of seasonal normal),\nwidespread showers and embedded thunderstorms will spread across\nthe region. Probabilities of 24-hour exceedance have increased \nthis run as the NBM percentages for 1 inch or more is 50 to 70%, \nwith a 30-50% chance of exceeding 2 inches, and a small but not \ninsignificant probability of 10-25% of exceeding 3 inches Friday \nand Saturday. Elevated instability remains limited with MUCAPEs \ngenerally under 500 J/kg, keeping the severe thunderstorm risk \nlow. \n\nForecast predictability decreases markedly Sunday into Monday \ndepending on how quickly the main western trough moves eastward, \nbut lingering mid-level energy will maintain a 20 to 30 percent \nchance of showers through Monday.",
